]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Two days after his gold medal in the giant slalom, " [:a {:href "/athletes/97080"} "Toni Sailer"] " won his second Alpine gold medal in the slalom, showing his dominance by posting the fastest time on each of the two runs. He would eventually win the gold medal by a full four seconds, Japan’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/87318"} "Chiharu Igaya"] " placing second. Igaya was the first Asian to win an Alpine skiing medal, or a Winter Olympic medal, although he had trained in the United States while a student at Dartmouth College. In 1982 Igaya would be named an IOC Member, eventually becoming a Vice-President of the IOC and serving 12 years on the Executive Board. Sweden’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/97686"} "Stig Sollander"] ", who had been third in the combined at the 1954 World Championships, also placed third here. Fourth place went to American " [:a {:href "/athletes/85238"} "Brooks Dodge"] ", the closest a US male Alpine skier had yet come to an Olympic medal."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {} "Run #1"] [:th {} "Run #2"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "15"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/97080"} "Toni Sailer"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUT"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUT.png"}] "AUT"]] [:td {} "3:14.7"] [:td {} "1:27.3 (1)"] [:td {} "1:47.4 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "7"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/87318"} "Chiharu Igaya"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JPN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/JPN.png"}] "JPN"]] [:td {} "3:18.7"] [:td {} "1:30.2 (6)"] [:td 